const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How often should a construction company post on LinkedIn?",
    a: "Once a week, held for a full year. Frequency matters far less than consistency, because buyers judge the pattern, and a page that posts weekly for a year reads as a company that finishes what it starts. If you want to do more, add volume after the weekly habit has survived a busy season, not before.",
  },
  {
    q: "What should we post when nothing exciting is happening?",
    a: "Post the plain explanation: how a panel gets stripped, why an embed sits where it sits, what curing means on a cold morning. This industry finds its own work interesting, and so do the people who buy from it. What feels routine inside the plant reads as substance to an estimator scrolling at a desk.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should posts come from the company page or from personal profiles?",
    a: "Both. A post shared by a person travels further than the same post from a page, so have owners and project managers reshare company posts with a sentence of their own. The company page is still the record. It is what a buyer checks during the desk review, so it needs the steady weekly history.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do hashtags matter on LinkedIn?",
    a: "No. LinkedIn's search reads the words of a post whether or not they carry a symbol in front of them, so a hashtag adds nothing a plain word does not. A pile of them reads as noise and marks the post as filler. Skip them and spend the effort on a clearer first sentence.",
  },
];
